($t10$617d9ebb-33a1-4121-bccd-2b8e3749ab62$t10$::uuid, $t10$Before measuring noise in an installed audio system, why should you first verify the integrity of its ground reference?$t10$),
($t10$05b068d9-3398-4f79-8c31-5d63cd1844fc$t10$::uuid, $t10$After confirming that a system's grounding and reference points sit at the expected potential, what does that mean for the measurements taken afterward?$t10$),
($t10$63b2d6e9-b2f4-4998-8365-472f6e997b44$t10$::uuid, $t10$After confirming a ground loop hum through a USB-connected interface, an engineer wants a permanent fix that does not depend on running the laptop on battery power. Which approach addresses the loop?$t10$),
($t10$cb9b701e-a520-491d-b1ba-cc961abd0493$t10$::uuid, $t10$Hum in a system is traced to current circulating between two devices that are each connected to earth. What does correctly identifying this as a ground loop allow the technician to do?$t10$),
($t10$90ff5080-59c3-4df4-8669-482746cde23d$t10$::uuid, $t10$A mix engineer notices playback glitching because too many plug-ins are running across several tracks at once. Which action is the most direct fix?$t10$),
($t10$bbd7b86d-8ac4-40d3-b676-d71560113c9f$t10$::uuid, $t10$During setup, an engineer needs a recorded bus to feed a hardware recorder instead of the main monitors. What should the engineer do?$t10$),
($t10$e4fd02e9-d0d8-4b36-8447-9756370ab803$t10$::uuid, $t10$During a live mix, an engineer wants the whole horn section to move together when they adjust one fader, keeping the balance between the horns consistent. What should the horn tracks be assigned to?$t10$),
($t10$fd6a13f8-1a58-4020-8642-8c58e0820260$t10$::uuid, $t10$At an outdoor concert, listeners near a distant delay tower speaker hear its sound arrive noticeably before the direct sound from the main PA reaches them. What is the correct fix?$t10$),
($t10$f0776ddf-5b3a-40fe-bf93-42567c4f50ee$t10$::uuid, $t10$Regarding industry connections, audio careers:$t10$),
($t10$2e03daaa-7cc3-4483-948c-7f93ca6c2f10$t10$::uuid, $t10$An A2 realizes mid-show that they sent the wrong monitor mix to a performer's wedge. What is the accountable response?$t10$),
($t10$b8439ef5-bb05-4221-a073-e5e42ca2ccbe$t10$::uuid, $t10$An engineer wants clients to keep discovering them on a professional networking platform over time. Which habit best supports ongoing discoverability?$t10$),
($t10$19af07b2-74e9-4177-a50c-c41c298ceb87$t10$::uuid, $t10$Which of the following is a safe manual-lifting technique?$t10$),
($t10$48e17199-4459-46a9-a81b-8cfb164fc855$t10$::uuid, $t10$Which of the following belongs in a basic first aid kit?$t10$),
($t10$b8d72d5f-9e5c-417b-8cc5-7900517893cf$t10$::uuid, $t10$Which of the following is part of inspecting a ladder before you climb it?$t10$),
($t10$bfe38f5d-c5a9-4269-852c-4ef358becffa$t10$::uuid, $t10$Which of the following is an example of Personal Protective Equipment (PPE)?$t10$),
($t10$f4db0698-90b1-4646-9932-e552396ce11b$t10$::uuid, $t10$Why is hidden hearing damage, or cochlear synaptopathy, described as hidden?$t10$),
($t10$fbd17648-cbeb-4cc2-8a37-f298c523fbd4$t10$::uuid, $t10$Which action is correct during an emergency evacuation?$t10$),
($t10$028c4d8b-8685-41f4-8798-69825f26c01d$t10$::uuid, $t10$A studio engineer is worried that high-sample-rate recordings containing ultrasonic content could cause equipment damage. Which pair of equipment problems are the recognized risks from ultrasonic content reaching the monitoring chain?$t10$),
($t10$3538fe34-ab3e-4b16-a215-7fcc95bdaafb$t10$::uuid, $t10$A safety officer logs several different sound levels across one crew member's shift and combines them into a single eight-hour time-weighted average. Why do that?$t10$),
($t10$f0655d9b-82c0-4dbf-925d-b6cfb72167ce$t10$::uuid, $t10$A show is beginning in a seated theatre while the audience is still settling into the rows. What is the standard house-light sequence at the top of the show?$t10$)) v(id,nt), (select count(*) as c from public.qq_t10_backup_20260903) g
where g.c = 399 and q.id=v.id and q.question_text is distinct from v.nt;

update public.quiz_questions q set explanation = v.nt
from (values ($t10$7516edd0-e3eb-4286-afcc-e64417f31bd6$t10$::uuid, $t10$Two or more ground paths joining interconnected devices form a closed loop, and the small circulating current at mains frequency couples into the audio as hum and buzz. The cure is to break the redundant path on the signal side, with a balanced interface or an isolation transformer, while every device keeps its own protective earth connected.$t10$),
($t10$459c2b3d-9256-436d-a0fc-bd35f52d93d2$t10$::uuid, $t10$A ground fault that energizes the chassis is a classic shock hazard. A standard overcurrent device does not respond to the few milliamps of leakage involved because that current is far below its trip threshold, not because it reacts too slowly. A GFCI is the device that senses the small line-to-neutral imbalance and cuts power fast enough to protect a person.$t10$),
($t10$2500d748-feb3-4574-9a30-fb0119aaa1b9$t10$::uuid, $t10$Short ACIDized loops already carry tempo metadata, so they stretch to the project tempo on their own. A long imported song has no such information until the Beatmapper measures its beats and writes a tempo map for it, so until then it plays back at its original speed instead of following the project.$t10$),
($t10$a117c931-045e-4572-a492-d2c662784c96$t10$::uuid, $t10$Half-speed record runs the transport at half speed so the guitarist can play the fast passage cleanly. Returned to normal speed the take plays at the intended tempo but one octave above what was played, so the player performs the passage an octave lower during the half-speed pass to land at the right pitch as well as the right tempo.$t10$),
($t10$04eadedc-e4a8-4750-ad8e-7a50a133a12a$t10$::uuid, $t10$On an alarm, stop work, treat the alarm as real, and leave by the planned route to the assembly area. The wrong answers are the three classic failures: hiding in place, silencing the alarm to keep working, and disabling a detector that keeps tripping on haze.$t10$),
($t10$07428430-a72f-4b44-9887-1f988813c628$t10$::uuid, $t10$PASS is Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep, so the second step is Aim, and it is aimed at the base of the flames rather than at the visible flame tips. Squeeze is a real PASS step offered in the wrong position, while Spray and Point are plausible sounding substitutes for Aim.$t10$),
($t10$15c99110-8c5d-4b9f-98f8-9c6036e0f305$t10$::uuid, $t10$A Safety Data Sheet is the standardized sixteen-section document supplied with a chemical, covering hazards, required protective equipment, safe use and storage, and spill and first-aid response, and it must be accessible to workers. The wrong answers recast it as manufacturer-only paperwork, an ingredient list stripped of hazard and first-aid information, or a permit needed to purchase the chemical.$t10$),
($t10$571e5130-bc88-4c7a-90ed-e22902ae6723$t10$::uuid, $t10$A pinch point is a catch, crush, or shear hazard where a hand or fingers can be caught between two converging objects, or between a moving object and a fixed one, such as a folding stand leg, a case lid, or stacked gear. The other options describe a knot in a coil, an uneven floor, and a spot prone to feedback, none of which crushes anything between two surfaces.$t10$),
($t10$6114df22-6260-40ae-a2a9-0e4bc108514a$t10$::uuid, $t10$Hearing protection lowers exposure, but real-world attenuation is consistently below the labeled NRR because fit in the field is poorer than in the laboratory, so wear it whenever levels are loud and sustained. The other options wrongly restrict protection to shop tools or to the mix engineer, or treat cotton balls as equivalent to rated earplugs, which they are not.$t10$),
($t10$8d43224b-88ae-4392-9404-90c747e7c557$t10$::uuid, $t10$Three of the four limbs stay on the ladder at all times, either two hands and one foot or two feet and one hand, whether climbing, descending, or working. The other options give the common two-point error, leaning the body on the ladder instead of gripping it, and a three-finger hold, which is not a point of contact.$t10$),
($t10$a5f8d754-e5aa-493c-8527-a4f97f103184$t10$::uuid, $t10$Call 911 in the US or Canada, and give the location, the nature of the emergency, and the number of people involved. Call early and assign one named person to make the call so it is not left to the crowd. The other three-digit numbers offered are directory assistance, a telecommunications relay service, and an emergency number used outside North America; none of them reaches a 911 dispatcher.$t10$),
($t10$bd02b626-0a92-42e7-acd0-dda63c4d252b$t10$::uuid, $t10$Electric shock is what happens when a person becomes part of an energized circuit so that current passes through the body. Severity tracks the magnitude of that current, the path it takes through the body, and how long it flows, which is why ordinary 120 V mains current can be fatal, and damp hands make it worse by lowering skin resistance. The distractors encode the how-long-it-has-been-switched-on, every-shock-is-fatal, and voltage-only-matters misconceptions.$t10$),
($t10$be10389d-2fee-44ba-a756-6a1189db0a5c$t10$::uuid, $t10$A near miss caused no harm this time but had realistic potential to do so, and it is reported and investigated like any other incident so the hazard behind it can be fixed before it does cause harm. All three distractors confuse a near miss with an event that actually produced an injury.$t10$),
($t10$de97e08b-b64c-41bd-819f-09caa31239bc$t10$::uuid, $t10$An incident is any unplanned event that caused harm or could have caused harm, including a near miss, and it is reportable either way. The wrong answers name signal and loudspeaker components, none of which is an event at all.$t10$),
($t10$e31855e1-eb98-484a-a949-25afc33fcc8a$t10$::uuid, $t10$Situational awareness means perceiving, understanding, and anticipating the surroundings in real time so a swinging load or a person nearing an edge is caught early enough to act. The wrong answers describe complacency about loads that may have moved, knowing a plan without watching current conditions, and head-down tunnel vision.$t10$),
($t10$ec66fd2b-734d-4c5e-807d-1ef1a26faf4d$t10$::uuid, $t10$Electrocution means death caused by electric shock, the worst outcome of contact and a leading but preventable cause of workplace fatalities. The wrong answers confuse it with a tripped overcurrent device, with any non-fatal shock, and with a harmless static tingle.$t10$),
($t10$f38d5c6b-7bcd-49a8-8227-e6c4ec42ef55$t10$::uuid, $t10$A team lift is coordinated: one leader, an agreed count, matched pace, and a clear route that keeps a heavy or bulky load level and under control. The wrong answers describe a solo carry with only an observer, a lift begun with no signal, and a lift with no leader and no shared timing.$t10$),
($t10$5ee1c83d-79d1-4825-beba-750cea552aba$t10$::uuid, $t10$The labeled NRR is a laboratory value, so it is adjusted before use: subtracting 7 dB converts it for comparison with A-weighted workplace levels, and halving the adjusted figure is then recommended because real-world fit is poorer than in the laboratory. Trusting the raw label, averaging two ratings, or ignoring the label entirely all give an unrealistic protection estimate.$t10$),
($t10$68d09463-93f7-43d4-a0cf-c40ee236b7ac$t10$::uuid, $t10$The correction is 7 decibels, and it applies whenever the workplace measurement is A-weighted rather than C-weighted. OSHA takes those 7 decibels off the protector's noise reduction rating and then halves what remains, so the estimated protected exposure in dBA equals the measured TWA minus (NRR minus 7) divided by 2. A 3 decibel figure is the exchange rate used by NIOSH, not this adjustment, and C-weighted levels take no such subtraction at all.$t10$),
($t10$83732609-abf2-444d-871d-b903a67ce570$t10$::uuid, $t10$With a 5 dB exchange rate the permitted duration halves for every 5 dB rise in level, so each minute spent in a louder environment consumes a larger share of the allowable daily dose.$t10$),
($t10$bb057b49-7003-4f36-aad9-a4d5f52a20f7$t10$::uuid, $t10$Power supplies and other circuits can hold a dangerous charge after the equipment is unplugged, so the tech pauses before making contact. The pause alone is not proof of safety: where large capacitors are involved, the charge must be confirmed gone with a meter and deliberately discharged if it is still present.$t10$)) v(id,nt), (select count(*) as c from public.qq_t10_backup_20260903) g
where g.c = 399 and q.id=v.id and q.explanation is distinct from v.nt;

update public.quiz_questions q set options_json = v.nt::jsonb
from (values ($t10$2ea93ed9-aec7-4431-bdb4-f874bae3e6ef$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Hiss contributed by the converter's own analog noise floor", "Clock jitter on the USB link that shifts sample timing", "Dropouts caused by lost packets on the USB bus", "Hum or buzz introduced when a USB link bonds two devices' grounds together, forming a ground loop"]$t10$),
($t10$3ae55113-d023-42c5-a34d-d70bdfbce613$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Thyristor light dimmers, switch-mode power supplies, and motor commutators", "Passive direct boxes and microphone splitter transformers", "Linear regulated supplies feeding analog preamplifiers", "Line-up tone generators"]$t10$),
($t10$aa5bef7a-f5cd-4530-b77f-4d9802af103b$t10$::uuid, $t10$["A steady low-frequency tone locked to the mains fundamental", "A single brief click at the onset of a signal", "A slow cyclic variation in level caused by supply ripple", "Spurious, broadband high-frequency electrical noise or interference"]$t10$),
($t10$b19fc29f-79e0-4491-8595-a94b4cc58caf$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Safety grounding must never be removed, because it carries fault current to protect people", "Safety grounding may be removed whenever it causes hum", "Safety grounding only matters for signal quality, not shock protection", "Safety grounding is only required on equipment drawing more than 1000 watts"]$t10$),
($t10$c7c55173-9519-4c25-ac6b-659f2c9bd9c3$t10$::uuid, $t10$["A linear regulated supply feeding a microphone preamp", "Switching power supplies, rectifiers, brush motors, or digital circuits", "A passive loudspeaker crossover network", "A correctly terminated balanced interconnect"]$t10$),
($t10$635bd8b4-f233-42f9-9cb0-646cd44f53b6$t10$::uuid, $t10$["That chassis, earth, and signal ground are connected and at the expected potential", "That the gain structure of the affected channel is optimized before anything else", "That the microphone cable polarity is correct at the stage box", "That the digital console is running at the intended sample rate"]$t10$),
($t10$8e033234-c52e-431f-a4b9-acce52d67e1e$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Using de-hum and spectral tools on the transfer to remove the switching hash content", "Re-transferring the same file at a higher sample rate", "Applying a low-cut filter at 80 Hz to the transfer", "Normalizing the transfer so the program sits above the noise"]$t10$),
($t10$a488782d-5e1c-4c2a-8700-66b3576c8bf4$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Article 647", "Article 250", "Article 210", "Article 645"]$t10$),
($t10$44c7a8cf-a0d5-42b1-8c2a-04d6b071c337$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Exactly four, regardless of how many parameters the track has", "None; automation is stored globally", "Only one, shared across all parameters", "One separate playlist per automatable parameter"]$t10$),
($t10$d96d983c-b50f-4893-b655-32c9d5d5774d$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Professionalism also requires reliability, ethics, respect, and appropriate communication, not just competence", "Professionalism is simply another word for technical competence", "Professionalism is measured solely by years on the job", "Professionalism excludes ethics and communication"]$t10$),
($t10$079b07b8-b924-4d9e-88ee-81a4bb43585e$t10$::uuid, $t10$["[\"Trip hazard\", \"Wet or slick footing that reduces traction\"]", "[\"Trip hazard\", \"The area beneath overhead work\"]", "[\"Trip hazard\", \"The chance that harm occurs and how severe it would be\"]", "[\"Trip hazard\", \"Cables or objects left in the walk path\"]"]$t10$),
($t10$0ad809f3-57b3-4760-8141-47571c1297b5$t10$::uuid, $t10$["The chance that harm occurs and how severe it would be.", "Only a danger that is obvious to an untrained observer.", "A written record filed after someone is injured.", "The danger source (e.g., a live wire or an overhead load)."]$t10$),
($t10$352fe67e-c84d-41ce-8a0d-7d896d1514b4$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Guess at the task so you look experienced in front of the crew.", "Attempt the task anyway and ask about it afterwards if something goes wrong.", "Refer every routine decision upward no matter how well trained you are.", "Confirm before acting on uncertain / high-hazard tasks."]$t10$),
($t10$58ad06a3-9c43-4e17-b19b-70e68f9ecfe6$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Anyone who happens to be on site when the work is scheduled.", "An untrained person working on energized equipment.", "Anyone who removes someone else's lockout device.", "The trained, designated persons permitted for the task."]$t10$),
($t10$58c40a18-dd7c-4548-af32-6425b270af94$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Stack cases top heavy so the light ones stay within reach", "Stand directly under a suspended load while it is being flown", "Move an unstrapped stack and trust it to stay put", "Keep the drop zone clear during overhead lifts"]$t10$),
($t10$63572153-e599-48fe-8416-f551b8165eb1$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Delay the call to 'handle it first'", "Move a person with a suspected spinal injury", "Crowd around the injured person to see what happened", "Only give care within your training"]$t10$),
($t10$63f54dc0-ca42-4180-a721-b6d3ec0f4dd9$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Water blocks current and forces it to travel through the equipment instead.", "Moisture that sharply raises electrical-shock risk.", "Damp air lowers the voltage delivered to amplifiers.", "Condensation affects audio quality only, not electrical safety."]$t10$),
($t10$6dac4e74-3e6a-4302-82a8-e7b79c77bbdf$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Take shortcuts whenever the schedule is tight", "Keep near misses quiet so nobody gets blamed", "Mock anyone who stops the job over a hazard", "Ask before acting when unsure"]$t10$),
($t10$705585b0-b62f-401a-b4b3-6d80382a60c6$t10$::uuid, $t10$["The idea that your responsibility ends at the boundary of your own task.", "The belief that following an unsafe order removes your responsibility.", "A duty that applies only after someone has already been injured.", "The obligation to avoid foreseeable harm to others."]$t10$),
($t10$727b1c6e-8bee-4eed-bb31-4cecce6bb947$t10$::uuid, $t10$["The source of harm itself, such as the object or condition.", "Judged by likelihood alone, ignoring severity.", "Judged by severity alone, ignoring how likely the harm is.", "Likelihood × severity of harm."]$t10$),
($t10$9237a6fd-cd41-4773-b36b-0085c87fe5eb$t10$::uuid, $t10$["coiled and returned to stock for reuse", "kept on the truck as a spare cable", "handed to the next crew with a verbal warning", "replaced"]$t10$),
($t10$9e8f8ec8-0e85-40a1-bf91-2021691741c6$t10$::uuid, $t10$["The absence of any accident so far.", "An optional step that mainly slows the job down.", "A one-time inspection completed at hire.", "Deliberate hazard control, not just 'no accident yet'."]$t10$),
($t10$a863bdb7-7a38-4936-95f6-0ca9a863ab9f$t10$::uuid, $t10$["[\"Class B\", \"Gasoline vapor but never liquid fuel\"]", "[\"Class A\", \"Any fire, regardless of class\"]", "[\"Class D\", \"Radioactive material fires\"]", "[\"Class B\", \"Flammable liquids\"]"]$t10$),
($t10$aeff40f6-d8d9-4e8d-b5d6-dbf61ac1c359$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Rising wind can be ignored while the structure is still standing.", "Banners and scrim can stay up in any wind because they are light.", "Ballast can be left off when a tower feels solid by hand.", "Wind's overturning/overload danger outdoors."]$t10$),
($t10$b5369395-052d-465a-87a2-4740e77b6f30$t10$::uuid, $t10$["[\"Heat cramps\", \"Dehydration cured instantly by salt tablets\"]", "[\"Heat exhaustion\", \"Sunburn blisters from UV alone\"]", "[\"Heat stroke\", \"Ringing in the ears from loud sound\"]", "[\"Heat rash\", \"Skin irritation from prolonged sweating\"]"]$t10$),
($t10$c394d32e-d01f-4fdd-9ad3-954645f29ed3$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Shelter under the nearest tall tree until the storm passes.", "Resume immediately once it stops raining.", "Keep working as long as no strike has been seen.", "As a lethal outdoor electrical-discharge hazard (shelter and wait)."]$t10$),
($t10$c7d8bd94-3ce1-4db2-91c3-fe917d850cd1$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Placing the heaviest cases at the top of a stack.", "Loading a truck/cart unevenly.", "Keeping a load top-heavy so it rides higher and more stably.", "Load-mass positioning for stability."]$t10$),
($t10$c924f772-e042-42d9-8aae-8acd1b64a5bd$t10$::uuid, $t10$["[\"Safety glasses\", \"Protects the feet from crushing loads\"]", "[\"Earplugs\", \"Protects the head from falling objects\"]", "[\"Gloves\", \"Protects hearing from high sound levels\"]", "[\"Hard hat\", \"Protects the head from overhead / falling objects\"]"]$t10$),
($t10$d8c57dc8-356d-431b-9c75-ad4c4697c1fe$t10$::uuid, $t10$["An exit chained or locked while the building is occupied.", "A single exit that is always sufficient.", "An exit with road cases stacked in front of it.", "A marked, unobstructed emergency egress."]$t10$),
($t10$dde42d8d-226b-4c18-a71b-cc1cc2249eb2$t10$::uuid, $t10$["A path that may be blocked with cases/cable/barricades.", "A single chokepoint everyone must funnel through.", "A path worked out on the spot once an emergency starts.", "The marked egress path to the assembly area."]$t10$),
($t10$252d1d64-743c-4616-9dd6-a8414022b8f0$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Fit a pad or sleeve where the sling bends over the corner to prevent it being cut", "Tension the sling harder so it cannot move against the corner", "Simply double the length of the sling", "Replace the sling with adhesive tape"]$t10$),
($t10$98cfd5de-9486-4412-9d78-8ed306b15072$t10$::uuid, $t10$["The monitor mix has too much reverb", "The occlusion effect from a well-sealed ear canal insertion", "Excessive stage volume bleeding into the monitors", "A blown driver in the in-ear monitor"]$t10$),
($t10$07589c54-99d4-4cc9-b39d-6a1af7024374$t10$::uuid, $t10$["A single short percussive click with no sustain", "Low-frequency mains hum and its harmonics, or a digital whine that changes with computer activity", "A broadband hiss whose level is unaffected by computer activity", "A high-pitched squeal that rises and falls with monitor gain, indicating acoustic feedback"]$t10$),
($t10$19aca9ec-17f1-4ffc-bc13-be42fe89144a$t10$::uuid, $t10$["Disconnect power from all the devices, then reconnect them one at a time until the hum appears, identifying the culprit", "Immediately lift every safety ground in the rack", "Raise the console fader until the hum is masked", "Replace all cables with unbalanced ones"]$t10$)) v(id,nt), (select count(*) as c from public.qq_t10_backup_20260903) g
where g.c = 399 and q.id=v.id and q.options_json is distinct from v.nt::jsonb;

update public.quiz_questions q set correct_answer = v.nt
from (values ($t10$98cfd5de-9486-4412-9d78-8ed306b15072$t10$::uuid, $t10$The occlusion effect from a well-sealed ear canal insertion$t10$),
($t10$19aca9ec-17f1-4ffc-bc13-be42fe89144a$t10$::uuid, $t10$Disconnect power from all the devices, then reconnect them one at a time until the hum appears, identifying the culprit$t10$)) v(id,nt), (select count(*) as c from public.qq_t10_backup_20260903) g
